<figure class="viz" id="viz-timeline">
  <p class="chart-title">From the sound to the moment you know</p>
  <div
    class="timeline-scroll"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Linear timeline from 0 to 300 milliseconds. A rat startle at about 8 milliseconds, a human eyeblink window from 20 to 80 milliseconds, and conscious recognition at roughly 200 milliseconds."
  >
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="timeline-track">
        <span class="timeline-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="tl-mark" style="left: 2.67%">
          <p class="tl-label lift pin-start">
            <strong>~8 ms</strong>
            <span>Rat startle. Davis, 1982.</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-window" style="left: 6.67%; width: 20%">
          <p class="tl-label">
            <strong>20–80 ms</strong>
            <span>Human eyeblink window. Blumenthal, 2005.</span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-mark" style="left: 66.67%">
          <p class="tl-label">
            <strong>~200 ms</strong>
            <span>Conscious recognition. A rough teaching estimate.</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    The flinch starts long before you know what you heard.{' '}
    <a href="https://www.jneurosci.org/content/2/6/791">Davis et al., 1982</a>,{' '}
